### Intersection of Sets

* **Definition:** The set formed by the `common elements of two or more sets` is called the intersection of sets.
* **Notation:** Denoted as $A \cap B$ and read as *"A intersection B"*.
* **Set Builder Form:** $A \cap B = \{x : x \in A \text{ and } x \in B\}$.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-5
![img](https://statisticseasily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/The-Union-and-Intersection-of-Two-Sets-3-1024x576.jpg)
:::
:::

**Example 9:** If $A = \{1, 3, 5\}$ and $B = \{5, 7\}$, 
then $A \cap B = \{5\}$.

**Example 10:** If $P = \{x : x \text{ is the multiple of } 2 \text{ and } x \le 8\} = \{2, 4, 6, 8\}$ and $Q = \{x : x \text{ is the multiple of } 4 \text{ and } x \le 12\} = \{4, 8, 12\}$, 
then $P \cap Q = \{4, 8\}$.
</md></sn><sn><md>:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-7
### Disjoint Sets

* **Definition:** If there is `no common element between two sets`, they are called disjoint sets.
* **Condition:** Sets $A$ and $B$ are disjoint if and only if $A \cap B = \emptyset$.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-5
![img](https://d138zd1ktt9iqe.cloudfront.net/media/seo_landing_files/venn-diagram-of-disjoint-sets-1627558994.png)
:::
:::


**Example 11:** If $A = \{x : x \text{ is odd natural number and } 1 &lt; x &lt; 7\} = \{3, 5\}$ and $B = \{x : x \text{ is factor of } 8\} = \{1, 2, 4, 8\}$, 
then $A \cap B = \emptyset$, meaning $A$ and $B$ are disjoint sets.

**Example 12:** If $C = \{3, 4, 5\}$ and $D = \{4, 5, 6\}$, 
then $C \cup D = \{3, 4, 5, 6\}$ and $C \cap D = \{4, 5\}$.
</md></sn>